 "standard_code": "Grade 1: Number and Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.deped.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/MATATAG-Mathematics-CG-Grades1-4-and-7.pdf",
 "standard_text": "Find the missing number in addition or subtraction sentences involving numbers up to 20; write an equivalent expression to a given addition or subtraction expression (e.g., 2 + 3 = 1 + 4; 10 − 5 = 6 − 1).",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 1 worksheets on missing numbers and equivalent expressions for the DepEd MATATAG curriculum: the equal sign, true or false, and missing numbers up to 20. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "= means the same as.",
 "key_sub": "Work out the side you know first.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>The equal sign means <b>the same as</b>. Both sides have the same value.</li><li>2 + 3 = 1 + 4 is true: both sides are 5.</li><li>To find a missing number, work out the side you know, then make the other side the same.</li><li>The box can be anywhere: ☐ + 4 = 9, 9 = ☐ + 4.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>6 + 4 = ☐ + 7</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6 + 4 = 10</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3 + 7 = 10</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Writing the left side's answer</td><td>In 6 + 4 = ☐ + 7, ☐ is not 10.</td></tr><tr><td>Answer after =</td><td>= means the same as.</td></tr><tr><td>Adding what you see</td><td>Make both sides equal.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does MATATAG Math 1 ask about missing numbers?",
   "a": "Find the missing number in addition or subtraction sentences involving numbers up to 20, and write an equivalent expression to a given addition or subtraction expression (for example, 2 + 3 = 1 + 4; 10 − 5 = 6 − 1)."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is 8 = 8 true?",
   "a": "Yes."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can the answer be on the left?",
   "a": "Yes: 9 = 4 + 5."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can I check?",
   "a": "Work out both sides."
  }
